    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the angle between <m>\vec{a}=\hat{i}+3\hat{j}+\hat{k}</m> and <m>\vec{b}=\hat{i}-2\hat{j}
            +\hat{k}</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <title>The Bird and The Train</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider two trains moving in opposite directions on the same track. The trains start simultaneously from
          two towns, Atown and Btown, separated by a distance <m>L</m>. Each train travels toward each other with 
          constant speed <m>v</m>. A bird is initially located in front of the train in Atown. As the train departs 
          Atown, the bird travels with speed <m>u \gt v</m> along the track towards Btown. 
        </p>

        <p>
          When it encounters the second train, it instantaneously reverses direction until it encounters the first
          train, then it reverses again, etc. The bird continues flying between the two trains until
          the trains meet, and then flies away. The purpose of this problem is to compute the total distance flown by 
          the bird until the trains meet. There are two good ways to solve this problem, and you can try either one. 
        </p>

 <p>       <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find an expression for the distance <m>d_n</m> covered by the bird after its n-th encounter with a 
              train. Define <m>d_0</m> as the distance traveled during the first flight from Atown towards the 
              train near Btown, <m>d_1</m> the distance traveled by the bird during the first trip from the Btown
              train to the Atown train, etc. You will get an infinite series to sum! 
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The other way is to look at the problem carefully, and realize that there is a very simple solution
              given all the assumptions here. If you take this route, remember to <alert>explain your 
                assumptions and steps clearly in the solution, don't just write equations.</alert>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul></p>
      </statement>
      
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
